  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-2}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
